\f0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 On a global map, a 5-meter sea level rise is invisible because the "stroke" of the coastline is thicker than the actual inundation. However, we can solve this by providing two different types of "Sea Level" data: the 
\f1\b Global Trend
\f0\b0  (The "Why") and the 
\f1\b Local Risk
\f0\b0  (The "What").\
What I found to populate those:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 1. The Global "Pulse" (Trend Data)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 To show the overall health of the planet, I\'92d prefer to use a 
\f1\b Line Chart
\f0\b0 . Apparently there is a tiny JSON or CSV file that updates automatically that we can fetch here:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Source:
\f0\b0  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://podaac.jpl.nasa.gov/dataset/NASA_SSH_GMSL_INDICATOR"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 NASA JPL Global Mean Sea Level (GMSL)}}\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Format:
\f0\b0  CSV/JSON\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Story:
\f0\b0  This shows the world average sea level rising at roughly 
\f1\b 3.4 mm/year
\f0\b0 . It\'92s the "Vital Sign" of the ocean.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 2. The Local "Inundation" (Visual Maps)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Since we can't show meters of rise on a world map because it would be mostly invisible or meaningless to people, we should use a 
\f1\b Tiled WMS
\f0\b0  that allows users to zoom into coastal cities (New York, Venice, Mumbai), where the pixels actually become visible.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Option A: The "Snapshot" Map (Historical Anomalies)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 If we want to show how the ocean is 
\f2\i currently
\f0\i0  higher than average:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Provider:
\f0\b0  
\f1\b Copernicus Marine Service (MyOcean)
\f0\b0 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Layer:
\f0\b0  
\fs26 Sea level gridded data from satellite observations
\fs24 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 WMS Endpoint:
\f0\b0  
\fs26 https://nrt.cmems-du.eu/thredds/wms/sea_level_global_phys_l4_nrt_observation_008_046
\fs24 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Benefit:
\f0\b0  This shows "Sea Level Anomaly" (SLA). It\'92s great for the 
\f1\b "History"
\f0\b0  tab because we can show areas where the sea is temporarily 20cm higher due to thermal expansion. \uc0\u8232 \u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Option B: The "Predictive" Map (User Interaction)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 If we want to allow users to move a slider (e.g., "+1m", "+5m"), we shouldn't use a WMS (which is a pre-rendered image). Can we use a 
\f1\b Digital Elevation Model (DEM)
\f0\b0 \outl0\strokewidth0 ?\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Get the Data:
\f0\b0  Use the 
\f1\b NOAA Sea Level Rise Viewer
\f0\b0  data or the 
\f1\b NASA/USGS CoastalDEM
\f0\b0 .\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Logic:
\f0\b0  We "filter" the pixels. If 
\fs26 Pixel_Elevation <= Slider_Value
\fs24 , we colour the pixel blue.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}
\f0\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Since rendering a global DEM in a browser is too heavy, we can use 
\f1\b Vector Tiles
\f0\b0  (MVT) for the coastline. I\'92ve learned that many developers use 
\f1\b Mapbox
\f0\b0  or 
\f1\b OpenMapTiles
\f0\b0  with a "bathymetry" layer to achieve this.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 3. How to make it "visible" at Global Scale\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 To solve the "pixels are too small" problem, we could use 
\f1\b Heatmaps
\f0\b0  or 
\f1\b Bubble Clusters
\f0\b0 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Instead of a blue line on the coast, place 
\f1\b Red Bubbles
\f0\b0  on major coastal cities.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Insight:
\f0\b0  The bubble size represents the "Population at Risk" for a 1-meter rise. (would it be possible to add a label, maybe on click?)\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 UX Win:
\f0\b0  When the user clicks the bubble, the map auto-zooms to that city and switches to the high-resolution inundation map where they can see the "pixels of land" disappearing.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Pro-Tip: The "Vulnerability" Insight\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 If we want to blow people's minds, correlate the 
\f1\b Land Cover
\f0\b0  data with the 
\f1\b Sea Level
\f0\b0  data.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 "This area in Florida was 40% 'Wetland' in 1992. Today, it is 80% 'Water'. This matches the 12cm sea level rise recorded at the nearest tide gauge."
\f0\i0 \
This turns a "boring" sea-level statistic into a "spatial proof" using existing ESA maps.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 1. The Dashboard Card Design\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 We should split the card into two sections: a 
\f1\b "Vital Sign"
\f0\b0  (the trend) and a 
\f1\b "Regional Alert"
\f0\b0  (the action).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Section A: The Global Vital Sign\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Headline:
\f0\b0  Global Mean Sea Level (GMSL)\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Metric:
\f0\b0  +3.4 mm/year (Current average)\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Context Snippet:
\f0\b0  
\f2\i "The ocean is currently rising at nearly double the rate of the 20th century. Since 1993, the global average has risen by ~10 cm."
\f0\i0 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Data Source:
\f0\b0  Use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://podaac.jpl.nasa.gov/dataset/NASA_SSH_GMSL_INDICATOR"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 NASA JPL/PO.DAAC Global Sea Level Indicator}}. They provide a standard CSV/JSON that we can parse to create a small sparkline chart similar to the "History" graphs.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Section B: The Local Risk (The Map Toggle)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Since we can't show the rise everywhere, we could use 
\f1\b "Hotspot"
\f0\b0  markers.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Visual:
\f0\b0  Place 10\'9615 pulsing blue circles on coastal mega-cities (e.g., Jakarta, Venice, Miami, Bangkok - which are the most r\outl0\strokewidth0 e\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 presentative?).\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Interaction:
\f0\b0  When a user clicks a hotspot, the card updates:\uc0\u8232 
\f1\b "Venice, Italy: Local Sea Level Trend"
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl1
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Relative Rise:
\f0\b0  +25cm since 1900.\
\ls6\ilvl1
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Cause:
\f0\b0  Combination of melting ice and 
\f1\b subsidence
\f0\b0  (the land is sinking while the water is rising).\
\ls6\ilvl1
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Visual:
\f0\b0  Trigger a map zoom that switches to the high-res ESA Land Cover layer to show the change in coastal wetlands.\
\ls6\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 When a user clicks one of these markers, we can use the existing 
\f1\b \strokec2 History
\f0\b0 \strokec2  tab logic to show a specific "Sea Level Anomaly" graph for that coordinate.\
\ls6\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}
\f1\b \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Pro Tip:
\f0\b0  Instead of just a pop-up, we should use the click event to 
\f1\b trigger a zoom
\f0\b0 . When the zoom level is $> 12$, swap the global low-res map for the 
\f1\b Copernicus Land Cover (100m)
\f0\b0  or 
\f1\b ESA WorldCover (10m)
\f0\b0 .\
\ls6\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
This will allow users to see exactly which neighbourhoods or coastal wetlands are transitioning from "Land" to "Water/Wetland" in the time-series.\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl1\cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 When adding POIs, it\'92s vital to distinguish between 
\f1\b \strokec2 Eustatic
\f0\b0 \strokec2  and 
\f1\b \strokec2 Relative
\f0\b0 \strokec2  sea level rise:\
\ls7\ilvl1
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Eustatic:
\f0\b0 \strokec2  The global average (mostly melting glaciers and thermal expansion).\
\ls7\ilvl1
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Relative:
\f0\b0 \strokec2  What people actually feel (Global rise + Land sinking).\
\ls7\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 By including the "Type" in the POI (like I did in the JSON), we can educate the user that "Climate Change" isn't the only culprit\'97human engineering and land use (like the ESA data shows!) play a huge role.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Since the dashboard is so sleek, I\'92d like to use 
\f1\b \strokec2 Pulsing Radials
\f0\b0 \strokec2 . A soft, semi-transparent blue pulse on these cities makes the map look "alive"\'97like the ocean is actually breathing against those coasts.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl1\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

