\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 3. The "Invisibility" Fix (Technical Tip)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 If we want a global map layer, use an 
\f1\b Anomaly Map
\f0\b0  instead of a 
\f1\b Sea Level Rise
\f0\b0  map.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Logic:
\f0\b0  Instead of showing "where the water is," we can show "how much higher the water is than the 1993 baseline."\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Visual:
\f0\b0  Areas of the ocean that are warmer (thermal expansion) will appear deep red/orange. Areas that are cooler appear blue.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Result:
\f0\b0  This makes the problem visible at a global scale because we are mapping 
\f1\b energy/volume change
\f0\b0  rather than just the coastline edge.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

